WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.930
This is a Wendover Productions video made
possible by Backblaze and in

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.120
collaboration with Alternate History Hub and
Real Life Lore.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:15.590
Russia is immense--it spans 5,000 miles across,
2,000 miles vertically12, crosses 11 time

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:19.700
zones3, borders everywhere from Norway to
North Korea4, and is as close to Anchorage

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.180
as it is
to Amsterdam5.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.760
It’s huge... but it has a problem.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.000
A problem that can explain part of why the
average Russian, living at the same latitude

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.050
as Finland, Sweden, Norway, Iceland, and Canada,
makes only $7,500 American dollars per year.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.290
A problem that can explain at least a portion
of

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:41.180
almost every political decision the country
has ever made.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.330
Russia’s geography is flawed.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:47.730
What you have to remember about Russia is
that the majority of Russians live in Europe.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.770
3/4 of Russia’s population lives in the
western quarter of the country6.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.191
Therefore, as a country
with a fairly centralized power system, many

00:00:55.191 --> 00:00:58.020
of its decisions go to protect the country
core in

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:59.020
and around Moscow.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:04.170
You see, a lot of the success of certain countries
over others depends on how well its

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.720
geography protects it.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:09.810
The US, for example, benefited hugely from
being an ocean away from

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.710
every large military power.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.840
The only real armed forces that could threaten
the US in its infancy

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:20.150
were in Europe and Asia and therefore any
invasion would require trans-oceanic supply

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.670
lines
which are hugely expensive and logistically

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:26.049
difficult therefore weakening an invading
army.

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:28.490
On
the European continent, France has a similar

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:31.060
situation—their northwestern border is protected
by

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:34.790
the English channel, their western border
by the Atlantic Ocean, their southern border

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:37.509
by the
Pyrenees mountains and Mediterranean ocean,

00:01:37.509 --> 00:01:39.560
their southeastern by the Alps mountains,
and

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.470
their northeastern by the Rhine river7.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.380
The eastern half of their northern border
is, however,

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:49.549
largely unprotected geographically—a flaw
Germany exploited in both World War One and

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:53.990
World War Two by invading through Belgium
and Luxembourg—but the protection still

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.670
did
concentrate attacks into a choke point and

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.110
kept the country significantly more protected
than

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:00.939
other European countries.

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:05.290
Here’s Cody from Alternate History Hub to
explain Russia’s territorial expansion.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:13.689
Russia’s first territorial expansion since
it first became a unified East Slavic State

00:02:13.689 --> 00:02:15.180
in 8828 was
entirely a quest for power.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.139
But over time this growth for glory transformed
into an effort to

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:20.950
protect the very core of the country.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.620
This early Russia was at very unprotected
and vunerable.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:29.689
There was no geographic protection to keep
foreigners from migrating into their lands.

00:02:29.689 --> 00:02:33.269
The only
natural resource Russia had their disposal

00:02:33.269 --> 00:02:34.730
to repel an invader was pure manpower.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.769
In the coming
centuries, what was then known as the Grand

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:40.439
Duchy of Moscow quickly expanded.

00:02:40.439 --> 00:02:43.560
By the time
Ivan the Terrible was crowned ruler of the

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.260
Tsardom of Russia, the country had spread
it’s borders

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:51.480
east to the Urals, south to the Caucasus mountains,
and west to the Carpathians.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.450
Soon even
Siberia was conquered which before then, was

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.920
an independent Khanate—a territory ruled
by a

00:02:58.920 --> 00:02:59.920
Khan.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.239
That was Cody from Alternate History Hub.

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:04.909
I collaborated with him to make a great
video on his channel about what the world

00:03:04.909 --> 00:03:09.169
would be like if Russia had never become its
own country which I’ll link in the description

00:03:09.169 --> 00:03:10.549
and at the end of the video.

00:03:10.549 --> 00:03:16.430
Now, with all this territory, Russia, or at
least Moscow, had some serious protection9.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:20.760
Siberia is large enough that no army could
invade through it and make it to Moscow.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.489
The supply
lines would have to be thousands of miles

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:26.269
long through inhospitable conditions.

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:29.290
Not only that,
but that army with a one or two thousand mile

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.629
long supply line would then have to make it
over

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:34.260
the Ural mountains to get to Moscow10.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.589
Attacking from the south or west would also
take an

00:03:36.589 --> 00:03:39.650
army either across water or through mountains.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:45.359
By the time the 19th century rolled in, Russia
had truly become an unconquerable power.

00:03:45.359 --> 00:03:48.379
Countries could and can take over portions
of Russia,

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:53.919
but there is no conceivable way that a single
country could fully occupy and conquer Russia.

00:03:53.919 --> 00:03:56.769
To
occupy a territory of that size, a country

00:03:56.769 --> 00:04:00.159
would need an estimated 13 million trained
ground

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:04.169
troops--more than the 17 largest militaries
combined11.

00:04:04.169 --> 00:04:09.559
However, despite its defenses, Russia has
never developed economically to the same level

00:04:09.559 --> 00:04:10.879
as some of its neighbors.

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:15.839
Its GDP per capita is
right around that of Mauritius, Grenada, and

00:04:15.839 --> 00:04:16.839
Turkey12.

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:21.709
And this, once again, can be at least
partially attributed to Geography.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:24.530
Historically, naval power equaled power.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.090
The two were synonymous.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:29.080
There was no better
way for countries to project their power and

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.780
grow their economy than to have a powerful
navy

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.210
and merchant fleet.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:37.060
Many of the most powerful countries today--the
United Kingdom, Japan, and

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:41.550
China for example--were ones that once had
the most powerful navies in the world.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.370
There’s a
reason that none of the 18 largest economies

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.919
in the world are landlocked countries13.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:50.110
Up until the
last century, maritime shipping was the fastest

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:53.199
way to get goods and people across the world
and

00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:56.580
its still cheapest way to ship goods long
distance.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.199
Having good water access allows countries
to

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:06.120
trade with the world but Russia, despite its
23 thousand miles of coastline14, has no significant

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.550
warm-water, ice-free ports with direct access
to an ocean.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:15.610
Alaska does, Canada does, Iceland
does, Norway does, and Sweden too, but Russia

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:21.110
is fundamentally limited in its maritime power
because it has no easy way to access the world’s

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:22.849
oceans year round.

00:05:22.849 --> 00:05:26.611
The port of Novorossiysk is
ice-free, but its throughput is limited both

00:05:26.611 --> 00:05:28.690
by the depth and size of the port15.

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:31.750
St Petersburg also
has an important port, but it freezes for

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.259
many months of the year.

00:05:33.259 --> 00:05:37.259
On the Pacific side, ports like
Vladivostok also occasionally freeze during

00:05:37.259 --> 00:05:38.259
the winter.

00:05:38.259 --> 00:05:40.740
But the ice is not the biggest problem
with these ports.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:44.169
The biggest problem is that their access to
the worlds oceans is all through

00:05:44.169 --> 00:05:48.189
choke-points controlled by either NATO countries
or NATO allies.

00:05:48.189 --> 00:05:51.009
To get to the ocean from
Novorossiysk, you need to pass through the

00:05:51.009 --> 00:05:53.409
Bosphorus straight which is controlled by
Turkey—a

00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:57.500
NATO country; to get to the ocean from St
Petersburg you need to pass through the Danish

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:01.509
straights controlled by Denmark—also a NATO
country16; and to get to the ocean from

00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:05.099
Vladivostok and many of the other Pacific
ports you need to pass through the sea of

00:06:05.099 --> 00:06:09.090
Japan which
is controlled by Japan—a close ally of NATO.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:14.999
If Russia ever decided to attack a NATO country,
their access to the oceans would be restricted

00:06:14.999 --> 00:06:19.860
by these NATO countries17 because the NATO
treaty includes a mutual defense pact—if

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.550
one country is attacked, all respond.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:26.210
This would cripple
both Russia's navy and economy.

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:28.139
Now, back to defenses.

00:06:28.139 --> 00:06:31.930
There’s one major flaw to Russia’s geographical
defense system--

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:33.990
the northern European plain.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:37.280
Whereas every other border has a geographical
defense preventing

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.840
easy invasion from a foreign army, this completely
flat plain just acts as a funnel easily bringing

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:47.880
an army from Western Europe right up to Moscow
. While 18 a large part of the Soviet Union’s

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.750
motive to expand into eastern Europe was to
spread the socialist revolution, Stalin still

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.120
believed
that he needed to create a zone of buffer

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:58.560
states in order to defend against the threat
of the USA

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.569
and its allies in Western Europe19.

00:07:00.569 --> 00:07:03.770
With its influence over all of Eastern Europe,
the USSR had

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:09.090
both manpower and political power to keep
the west far from Moscow.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.520
Since the fall of the
Soviet Union, Russia has continued to strive

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:14.860
to keep political power in the region.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.870
Out of the 15
states that emerged from the Soviet Union,

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:21.120
12 joined a Commonwealth of Independent States
with Russia20--essentially aligning them politically

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:26.729
with Russia--while three joined both NATO
and the European Union--Lithuania, Latvia,

00:07:26.729 --> 00:07:28.010
and Estonia.

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:33.620
That means that, on paper, Russia still
had a strong political buffer between it and

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:34.620
western Europe.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:38.870
The Russian exclave of Kaliningrad,
and the countries of Belarus and Ukraine covered

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.660
almost all of the Northern European Plain.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:46.379
Not only that, but Russia used its influence
in Ukraine to sign a long term lease on the

00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:49.620
warmwater
port of Sevastopol which greatly expanded

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.940
the naval capabilities of Russia’s black-sea
fleet21.

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:58.110
Except, Ukraine as a whole progressed to be
more and more pro-European in the decades

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:03.180
following the fall of the Soviet Union which
was a major reason for Russia’s invasion

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.510
of Crimea.

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:08.240
While on the surface Putin might have claimed
Russia’s invasion was to save the Russians

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.090
of the
area from the increasingly westernizing country,

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:16.719
the annexation of Crimea was in reality a
strategic imperative to keep warm-water port

00:08:16.719 --> 00:08:17.719
of Sevastopol.

00:08:17.719 --> 00:08:21.661
A Ukraine that was friendlier to the
west likely would have ended Russia’s lease

00:08:21.661 --> 00:08:24.669
on the port so in Putin’s mind, he needed
to invade

00:08:24.669 --> 00:08:29.580
Crimea in order to prevent a crippling blow
to Russia’s ocean access.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:34.260
Now, Russia has managed to overcome many of
its geographic challenges partially

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:37.680
because of two things--oil and natural gas.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:41.099
It has enormous energy reserves partially
because of

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:42.810
its enormous size.

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:47.040
Russian natural gas pipelines provide for
40% of Europe’s natural gas

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.100
demand.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:53.100
Some countries such as Bulgaria, Lithuania,
Latvia, Estonia, and Finland are almost

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:58.220
fully dependent on Russia for their natural
gas . This 22 gas dependency is a major reason

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.760
why
Germany, for example, a country with high

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:03.480
Russian oil dependency, is much less likely
to

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:09.149
criticize Russia than a country like the UK,
which has virtually zero Russian gas dependency.

00:09:09.149 --> 00:09:11.550
If
Russia shut off the gas to Germany, it would

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:14.680
devastate them, but stopping gas exports to
the UK

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:16.270
would have little effect.

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:19.390
The US has attempted to reduce Russian influence
in Europe by

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:22.649
exporting liquefied natural gas across the
Atlantic.

00:09:22.649 --> 00:09:27.769
It costs more, but it allows western european
countries to buy their energy from their American

00:09:27.769 --> 00:09:28.769
ally.

00:09:28.769 --> 00:09:32.279
Now, none of this discussion of ports and
power is to say that if there was no Norway

00:09:32.279 --> 00:09:34.250
or
Sweden blocking the way to the ocean and the

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:37.500
water was a bit warmer Russia would be the
Sweden of the East.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:39.340
Saying that would be foolish.

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:43.500
Geography does have an enormous influence
on human development, but it doesn’t determine

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:44.500
it.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:49.560
Much of history is defined by chance, not
circumstance because, in the end, reality

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:53.300
is just the confluence of chance and circumstance.

